findings.finding:
I replayed every vector against 285 mutants: each require, and each top-level conjunct, disabled in turn, plus 8 hand mutants of the command-line and tree rules. Every whole v3 require is caught, except the schema pins (an exception the README lists) and one other: 'migration inputs', which v3 changed to take v2_records. It is a type guard that a JSON case cannot reach, and the README does not list it among the exceptions. These halves are not pinned by any case:
(a) the rd.systemd.unit= half of the enrolled-entry rule. Probe N9 is refused by the model, but dropping that half changes no case;
(b) the v1_records half of 'attested empty history with earlier records'. M09 uses v2 records only.
V15, V16 and V17 add a census entry but keep sliceDescendants at 7. With the pinned rule disabled, each is still refused by the parent rule or by 'slice descendants differ from the census'. This is the shape R2-4(c) fixed for L11, L12 and L14.
Two cases are double by construction:
- L14: any path outside the slice also lacks a listed parent;
- V42: equal command lines always break one of the two token rules, so the token rules already imply the command-line half of the 'indistinguishable' rule.
The three rules R2-4 named are now pinned:
- the path-to-inode half, by V56;
- the enrolled-cgroups rule, by V41;
- the descendant count, by V50.

R2-1.note:
Done:
- " is refused by the schema;
- lockdown stays a kernel-parameter test before the first bare '--';
- the enrolled rule reads every word, including rd.systemd.unit=;
- the maintenance rule uses the last systemd.unit=.
Re-run probes: W6-a is refused 'enrolled boot entry names the maintenance target' (V52). W6-b and W6-c are refused by the schema pattern (V54). W6-d is accepted (A08). W6-e is refused 'maintenance boot entry does not select the maintenance target' (V53).
Not done: systemd also unquotes ', and runlevel words take part in last-wins (R3-1). The optional SystemdOptions limit is now listed in the K1 statement.

R2-4.note:
The required changes are present:
- V56 pins 'one file reported at two inodes', and that message is now distinct;
- V41 sets sliceDescendants to 6 and pins 'planeon slice census incomplete';
- V50 pins 'slice descendants differ from the census', and that message is now distinct;
- L11, L12 and L14 raise sliceDescendants to 8.
The mutation replay confirms each of these rules is caught. Residuals are in R3-2: V15-V17 are double-defect, two halves have no vector, and one exception is not stated.

questionAnswers.Q2:
Every v2 rule still holds.
- All 131 v2 negatives replay. Their refusals differ from v2 only in R01 (mechanical) and in R70, R71, L13 and Q07, the four README cases, each re-checked.
- Ops change only in C14-C18, C20 (threadCount and an unenrolled image), Q03 (a v3 capture), L11, L12, L14 (descendant count) and A02.
- Identity disjointness is still subsumed: the enums are disjoint and the KUBELET pattern matches no other name.
Legitimate hosts accepted:
- A05, A06, A07 and A08;
- a nested unpopulated leftover with a matching count;
- the gate on another device with the same inode number;
- libpython or libssl shared by roles outside the tree, or by SERVER and WORKER under it;
- a SMBIOS extra string;
- lockdown=none after lockdown=integrity.
Legitimate configurations refused:
- any " in the command line, for example a bootconfig value with spaces, which init/main.c quotes. This is by design.
- a native role library byte-identical to a library bundled in the Python tree (R3-3).
- a MAINTENANCE entry that names its target only in quotes (conservative).

questionAnswers.Q4:
Yes, as a loader-reported, unmeasured discriminator. It meets W02e K1's wording, which accepts a record-level command-line discriminator or an explicit trusted-installer statement, and v3 gives both.
The README statement is accurate:
- the entries are declared;
- LoaderEntrySelected is set volatile with runtime access by systemd-boot v256;
- /proc/cmdline is fixed for the boot and includes bootconfig keys and init arguments;
- without Secure Boot, SystemdOptions can supply systemd.unit=;
- nothing is measured. systemd-boot v256 does log load options to the TPM (tpm_log_load_options), which T04 may use, but the record does not rely on it.
It is sufficient for telling an honest maintenance boot apart. It is incomplete only where the token rules misread systemd (R3-1): an ENROLLED line can still select the maintenance target through single quotes and pass.

questionAnswers.Q5:
Refused as intended:
- the gate at the broker's inode;
- one file at two inodes;
- a content copy of another role's script;
- a content copy of a tree file in a native role;
- a cgroup below a role cgroup;
- a third broker child;
- a nested leftover without its parent;
- a worker-domain process outside its cgroup;
- a capture on the maintenance entry;
- a wrong command-line hash;
- same entry IDs;
- every replayed v1/v2 boot or nonce reuse.
Accepted, and contrary to the contract's intent:
- ENROLLED lines that select the maintenance target through single quotes (N1-N3);
- MAINTENANCE lines overridden by a later quoted unit or a runlevel word (N5-N8). Both are R3-1.
Accepted as stated residuals:
- interpreted roles' scripts (declared, not observed);
- a Python runtime outside the tree;
- a fabricated unpopulated census entry with a matching count (inspector honesty).
I found no way for a bundle to carry its own observation of a foreign cgroup, process or program and still qualify. Cgroup paths and inodes, (pid, start ticks), (device, inode) and program IDs stay bound and unique.

questionAnswers.Q6:
Each case produces exactly its pinned result in my independent replay, which rebuilds every case from fresh JSON copies:
- the 4 positives are accepted with test_fixture=True under full, v1-only, v2-only and attested-empty histories, and are refused 'test-only implementation profile' without the flag;
- 187/187 negatives, of which 50 are schema-level refusals, each a single error;
- 8/8 accepted variants;
- 14/14 cross-version cases;
- 13/13 migration cases.
The intents of V52-V56 and A08 match the rules that fire. V15-V17 carry a second defect (R3-2).
The positives are now kernel- and loader-plausible: initrd= comes first, the type #1 entry IDs are file names, every inode names one path with nlink 1, and the counts are consistent.

questionAnswers.Q7:
Nothing claims a native observation, a measured boot, a selected distribution, seccomp filters or an E01-E12 proof. The 'Not claimed' section is intact.
Wording that claims more than the bytes support:
- the K1 row's 'A command line may not contain ", so splitting on spaces gives the words the kernel and systemd see';
- 'systemd reads every word ... and the last systemd.unit= wins', together with decision 2 and the model comment at line 243 (all R3-1);
- the 'Files' mutation claim, which has one unlisted exception (R3-2).

extraProbes.observed:
